Authority-Constrained Agent Harnesses: 

A Mathematical Theory of Separated Authority

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 


Abstract

We develop a mathematical theory of authority for AI agent systems in which reasoning capability and execution authority are formally separable objects. The central structural claim is that the recurring failure mode of contemporary agent frameworks is unconstrained authority propagation across trust boundaries — a structural recurrence of the confused deputy problem (Hardy 1988; CWE-441) in a new medium.

We introduce the state space $\mathcal{S}_t = (I_t, C_t, M_t, X_t, E_t, P_t, \Pi_t, \Lambda_t)$ with reasoning state $I_t$, context $C_t$, memory $M_t$, execution authority $X_t$, execution environment $E_t$, principal provenance $P_t$, policy state $\Pi_t$, and approval ledger $\Lambda_t$. We separate explicitly granted authority $X^{\exp}_t$ from ambient authority $X^{\text{amb}}_t = X_t \setminus X^{\exp}_t$ and state the design objective $I \uparrow$ while $X^{\text{amb}}_t \cap \mathrm{Priv} = \varnothing$.

We introduce a capability lattice $(\mathcal{X}, \preceq)$ with monotone delegation $D : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{P} \to \mathcal{X}$ satisfying $D(x,p) \preceq x$, and prove the Delegation Non-Amplification Theorem: no chain of delegations can exceed the originating grant.

We formalize approval as certificate, not boolean. An approval is a tuple $\alpha$ binding principal, agent, tool, canonical arguments, resource handle, execution context, provenance chain, validity window, policy version, capability identifier, nonce, audience, one-time-use flag, and (conditionally) file-operand binding. We prove a Sufficiency Theorem characterizing when the field map of $\alpha$ is a sufficient statistic for the effect set of the execution.

We derive five core invariants I₁…I₅ and three design principles DP1…DP3, each invariant defined as a predicate on states. We prove the ACAH enforcement topology satisfies them by construction, under complete mediation.

We state and prove a Topological Noninterference Theorem in the Goguen–Meseguer tradition: runs differing only in low-integrity inputs produce identical privileged-effect traces except at explicit ratification events.

We construct a witness set $\mathcal{W}$ of published advisories establishing reachability of each invariant violation, without statistical claim.

We identify six residuals as open mathematical problems.

