Chapter 1 — Introduction

1.1 Motivation

Contemporary AI agent frameworks converge on a common architecture:

\text{LLM} + \text{Memory} + \text{Tools} + \text{OS} + \text{Network} + \text{Messaging}

Each additional capability expands the trust boundary. The reasoning model is placed near — or inside — the authority domain of the host operating system.

The 2026 literature has converged on a structural diagnosis: untrusted content acts with authority it was never granted. The SoK on agentic AI security surveys 71 papers (2022–2026) and three production CVEs, organizing prior work into a five-layer taxonomy (input, external data, tool and protocol, memory, multi-agent) and identifying the common structural root: "untrusted content acts with authority it was never granted, which we formalize as the violation of four security invariants". The multi-agent layer is identified as "both the most dangerous and least defended: current protocols track only the calling agent, not the user whose authority is delegated, letting authority be laundered through a trusted intermediary".

This is the confused deputy problem (Hardy, 1988; CWE-441) in a new medium: a program holding legitimate credentials is tricked into using its own authority on someone else's behalf. The essential insight, as Hardy formulated it, is that authority should be carried by the invoker of an action, not merely held in ambient form by the program performing the action.

AI agents are structurally excellent deputies: they are intermediaries by design, they hold broad credentials, and they treat all content in their context window as potentially instructive. When an agent holds OAuth tokens, API keys, and shell execution ability, every input channel becomes a potential attack vector.

The prevailing response has been to add guardrails: prompt instructions, allowlists, sandboxing heuristics. We argue, mathematically, that this is structurally insufficient because it treats security as an additive property rather than a topological property of the system.

1.2 Thesis

The security of an agentic system is determined not by the intelligence of its model, nor by the strength of any individual guardrail, but by the topology of its authority boundaries.

We propose to replace the naive pipeline

\text{LLM} \to \text{Decision} \to \text{Tool} \to \text{OS}

with a constrained topology

\text{LLM} \to \text{Intent} \to \text{Policy} \to \text{Authorization} \to \text{Sandbox} \to \text{Tool} \to \text{OS}

and to formalize the invariants any such topology must satisfy.

1.3 Contributions

1. A state-space model $\mathcal{S}_t = (I_t, C_t, M_t, X_t, E_t, P_t, \Pi_t, \Lambda_t)$ for authority in agentic systems.
2. A capability lattice $(\mathcal{X}, \preceq)$ with monotone delegation and an out-of-model minting operator, with non-circular definition of explicit vs. ambient authority.
3. A Delegation Non-Amplification Theorem (Theorem 5.4).
4. An approval-as-certificate formalism with a Sufficiency Theorem (Theorem 8.3) characterizing when field equality implies semantic containment.
5. Five core invariants I₁…I₅ defined as predicates on $\mathcal{S}$, and three design principles DP1…DP3.
6. A Topological Noninterference Theorem (Theorem 7.6) in the Goguen–Meseguer tradition.
7. A witness set $\mathcal{W}$ demonstrating reachability of each invariant violation.
8. Six residuals as open mathematical problems.

1.4 Methodological Discipline

We maintain strict separation between:

· Layer A — Definitions, axioms, and theorems (ours).
· Layer B — Cited results (classical and 2026 literature).
· Layer C — Witnesses (published advisories, used only for existence proofs).

No Layer B result is used to prove a Layer A theorem. No Layer C witness is used as a prevalence claim.

1.5 Positioning

This dissertation does not claim to discover the confused deputy, transitive trust violations, or data-to-code promotion. These patterns are named and analyzed in the classical literature (Dennis–Van Horn, HRU, take-grant, Miller) and in the 2026 literature (SoK, PEDIGREE, CaMeL, FIDES, approval laundering). The contribution is a mathematical integration:

\boxed{\text{taxonomy} \to \text{invariants as predicates} \to \text{noninterference} \to \text{sufficiency} \to \text{proofs}}

