Chapter 2 — Threat Model and Boundary Topology

2.1 Trust Boundary

Definition 2.1 (Trust Boundary). A trust boundary is a locus in a system where the authority governing an action may change hands.

In an agentic system, boundaries exist between:

1. Model → Context
2. Context → Intent
3. Intent → Policy
4. Policy → Capability
5. Capability → Execution
6. Execution → Observation
7. Observation → Memory
8. Memory → Context
9. Agent → Agent
10. Human → System

Definition 2.2 (Boundary Violation). A boundary violation occurs when authority crosses a boundary without being re-derived at the destination. This is the unifying abstraction of the dissertation and the structural form of the confused deputy problem: an unmitigated path from an untrusted source to a privileged sink.

2.2 Native Threat Model (Adopted)

We adopt for the primary theory the local-first, single-operator threat model:

· The system is local-first for trusted operators.
· Multi-tenant scenarios are out of scope for the primary theory.
· Prompt-injection-only scenarios are not by themselves violations of I₁…I₅.
· The Gateway is the local control plane; models and harnesses are interchangeable.
· Agents share a trust boundary; an operator capable of operating an agent can make it do what the operator can do.

Remark 2.3. The multi-agent layer is identified in the 2026 SoK as "both the most dangerous and least defended". Adoption of the single-operator threat model renders the primary theory structurally blind to this layer. We retain this as R₃ (Multi-Agent Amplification) and address it formally as an open problem in Chapter 10.

2.3 Extension Threat Model

We define $\mathrm{TM}_2 = \mathrm{TM}_1 + \text{multi-agent}$. Chapter 10 states the multi-agent problem formally as a residual.

