Chapter 3 — Formal Framework

3.1 State Decomposition

Definition 3.1 (System State). At time $t$, an agentic system is modeled by

\boxed{\mathcal{S}_t = (I_t, C_t, M_t, X_t, E_t, P_t, \Pi_t, \Lambda_t)}

where:

Symbol Meaning Design constraint
$I_t$ Reasoning state Maximize capability
$C_t$ Context Label integrity
$M_t$ Memory Provenance-constrained
$X_t$ Execution authority $X^{\text{amb}}_t \cap \mathrm{Priv} = \varnothing$
$E_t$ Execution environment Semantically authorized
$P_t$ Principal / delegation provenance Non-forgeable chain
$\Pi_t$ Policy / enforcement state Canonical
$\Lambda_t$ Approval ledger Nonce-uniqueness

The ledger $\Lambda_t$ is added to support the Verify predicate's nonce and one-time-use checks. Without it, replay attacks are not ruled out at the type level.

Definition 3.2 (Privileged Capability Set). Fix a deterministic predicate $\mathrm{IsPrivileged}$ over capability attributes. Then $\mathrm{Priv} = \{ \kappa : \mathrm{IsPrivileged}(\kappa) = 1 \}$.

Definition 3.3 (Capability Metric). Fix a benchmark $B$ and task distribution $\mathcal{T}$. Define $I_t = \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \mathcal{T}}[\mathrm{Perf}(B, \tau)]$.

3.2 Authority as Time-Indexed Capability

Definition 3.4 (Capability). A capability is a tuple

x = (r, a, s, \mathrm{TTL}, \pi, \mathrm{id}_X, n, \mathrm{aud}, \mathrm{otp}, \mathrm{rev})

where $r$ is a resource handle (not a name), $a$ is an action, $s$ is scope, $\mathrm{TTL}$ is time-to-live, $\pi$ is provenance, $\mathrm{id}_X$ is a capability identifier, $n$ is a nonce, $\mathrm{aud}$ is audience, $\mathrm{otp}$ is a one-time-use flag, and $\mathrm{rev}$ is a revocation handle.

Definition 3.5 (Explicit Authority — Non-Circular). At time $t$, a capability $x \in X_t$ is explicitly granted, written $x \in X^{\exp}_t$, iff there exists a provenance chain

\pi(x) = (\mathrm{Grant}(\rho), D(x_1, p_1), \ldots, D(x_k, p_k))

rooted in a trusted Grant event $\mathrm{Grant}(\rho)$ where $\rho$ is a policy-derived source, and every subsequent element is a monotone delegation. Capabilities not satisfying this condition are ambient:

X^{\text{amb}}_t = X_t \setminus X^{\exp}_t.

Remark 3.6. The host process's own privileges (e.g., the shell's UID, the network stack's ambient capabilities) are included in $X_t$ as ambient by Definition 3.5, since they are not rooted in a trusted Grant event within the model.

Definition 3.7 (Ambient-Authority Design Principle).

\boxed{\text{DP2}:\quad X^{\text{amb}}_t \cap \mathrm{Priv} = \varnothing.}

DP2 is a design principle, not a theorem: it constrains the class of admissible architectures.

3.3 Capability Lattice

Definition 3.8 (Facet Vector). Model authority as an element of a product lattice over seven facets:

x = (s_{\text{scope}}, s_{\text{spend}}, s_{\text{depth}}, s_{\text{time}}, s_{\text{rep}}, s_{\text{values}}, s_{\text{rev}}).

Definition 3.9 (Authority Order). Define $x_1 \preceq x_0$ iff for every facet $f$, $x_1[f]$ contains no more authority than $x_0[f]$. This is a partial order.

Proposition 3.10. $(\mathcal{X}, \preceq)$ is a bounded lattice with bottom $\bot$ (no authority) and top $\top$ (unrestricted authority).

Proof. Each facet is a bounded lattice under its narrowing rule: scope narrows by coverage, spend by decrease, depth by decrease, time by shortening (with $\infty$ top), reputation by tightening, values by adding requirements (reversing the order relative to raw value sets), reversibility by increasing the reversibility class. Finite product of bounded lattices is a bounded lattice; componentwise meet and join are well-defined. $\square$

3.4 Delegation

Definition 3.11 (Delegation). A delegation is a map $D : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{P} \to \mathcal{X}$ required to satisfy

\boxed{D(x, p) \preceq x \quad \forall x, p.}

Definition 3.12 (Renewal). Renewal is a distinct operation: $\mathrm{Renew}(x, \rho) = \mathrm{Grant}(\rho)$.

Axiom 3.13 (Authority Minting). For every transition from $t$ to $t+1$:

\boxed{\forall x' \in X_{t+1} \setminus X_t,\ \exists x, p:\ x' = D(x, p) \lor x' = \mathrm{Grant}(\rho).}

Axiom 3.14 (Model Non-Minting).

\boxed{\mathrm{Grant}(\rho) \notin I_t.}

3.5 Approval as Certificate

Definition 3.15 (Approval Certificate). An approval is a tuple

\boxed{\alpha = (p, A, T, \text{args}_{\mathrm{canon}}, r_{\text{handle}}, s, (cwd, \mathrm{env\_hash}, \mathrm{node\_id}), \pi_{\mathrm{chain}}, t_0, t_1, v_P, \mathrm{id}_X, \mathrm{fb}^{?}, n, \mathrm{aud}, \mathrm{otp})}

where $r_{\text{handle}}$ is a resource handle (not a name) and $\mathrm{fb}^{?}$ is the optional file-operand binding.

Definition 3.16 (File Binding Predicate). For every tool $T$ and argument tuple $\text{args}$, define a policy predicate $\mathrm{RequiresFileBinding}(T, \text{args})$.

Definition 3.17 (Verify Predicate with Ledger). Execution $e$ at time $t$ under ledger state $\Lambda_t$ is authorized iff

\boxed{\mathrm{Verify}(\alpha, e, t, \Lambda_t) = 1 \iff \begin{cases} t \in [t_0, t_1] \\ \mathrm{Canonicalize}(\text{args}_e) = \text{args}_{\mathrm{canon}} \\ cwd_e = cwd_\alpha \\ \mathrm{hash}(\mathrm{env}_e) = \mathrm{env\_hash} \\ \mathrm{node}_e = \mathrm{node\_id} \\ r_e = r_{\text{handle}} \\ \exists\, f:\ \mathrm{hash}(f_e) = \mathrm{fb}\ \text{if}\ \mathrm{RequiresFileBinding}(T, \text{args}_e) \\ \pi_e \text{ extends } \pi_\alpha \text{ monotonically} \\ \mathrm{PEP\ enforces\ PDP\ decision\ under}\ (v_P, \mathrm{id}_X) \\ n \notin \Lambda_t \\ \mathrm{aud}(\alpha) = p \\ \mathrm{otp}(\alpha) = 0 \lor \text{first use} \end{cases}}

Remark 3.18. The predicate $\mathrm{PEP\ enforces\ PDP\ decision}$ is defined formally in Definition 6.3: the PEP's output transition is the identity on the PDP's decision, i.e., $\mathrm{Out}_{\mathrm{PEP}}(q) = \mathrm{Out}_{\mathrm{PDP}}(q)$ for every request $q$.

3.6 Authorization Function

Definition 3.19 (Authorization Function). A request $q$ to execute action $a$ on resource $r$ by principal $p$ is authorized iff

\mathrm{Allow}(q) = \Pi(p, X, r, C_{\text{integrity}}, \pi, E, \mathrm{Risk}, t).

Axiom 3.20 (Fail-Closed).

\boxed{\mathrm{Unknown} \Rightarrow \mathrm{Deny}.}

3.7 Architecture and Invariants

Definition 3.21 (Architecture as Transition). An architecture $\mathfrak{A}$ induces a transition $\mathfrak{A} : \mathcal{S}_t \times q_t \to \mathcal{S}_{t+1}$. Let $\mathrm{Reach}(\mathfrak{A})$ denote the set of reachable states.

Definition 3.22 (Invariant Satisfaction). An invariant $I_k : \mathcal{S} \to \{0,1\}$ is satisfied by $\mathfrak{A}$ iff

\boxed{\mathfrak{A} \models I_k \iff \forall s \in \mathrm{Reach}(\mathfrak{A}): I_k(s) = 1.}

A violation is a reachable state $s^*$ with $I_k(s^*) = 0$.

3.8 Reference Monitor Axioms

Axiom 3.23 (Complete Mediation). Every effect passes through the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).

Axiom 3.24 (Tamperproofness). The PEP, PDP, and Intent Compiler cannot be modified by any computation in the cognitive loop.

Axiom 3.25 (Verifiability). The PEP, PDP, and Intent Compiler are small enough to be analyzed and tested.

These are Anderson's three reference monitor criteria, stated as axioms. Without Axiom 3.23, Theorem 7.6 is false.

3.9 Effect Semantics

Definition 3.26 (Effect Vocabulary). Fix a finite set $\mathcal{E}$ of effect primitives (e.g., read, write, exec, net, spawn, delete). The effect set of an execution $e$ is a subset $\mathrm{Eff}(e) \subseteq \mathcal{E}$.

Definition 3.27 (Semantic Containment). Execution $e$ is semantically contained in approval $\alpha$, written $\mathrm{Eff}(e) \subseteq \mathrm{Eff}(\alpha)$, iff every effect primitive realized by $e$ is authorized by $\alpha$.

Remark 3.28. The effect set is a finite object, so containment is decidable. This removes the Rice's-theorem obstacle of the third draft. The difficult problem is not decision but characterization: what field configurations of $\alpha$ are sufficient to determine $\mathrm{Eff}(e)$?

