Chapter 5 — The Delegation Non-Amplification Theorem

Definition 5.1 (Delegation Chain). A delegation chain of length $k$ is a sequence $x_0 \xrightarrow{p_1} x_1 \xrightarrow{p_2} \cdots \xrightarrow{p_k} x_k$ with $x_i = D(x_{i-1}, p_i)$.

Lemma 5.2 (Monotone Composition). For any $x \in \mathcal{X}$ and any $p_1, p_2 \in \mathcal{P}$: $D(D(x, p_1), p_2) \preceq x$.

Proof. By DP3, $D(x, p_1) \preceq x$. By DP3 again, $D(D(x, p_1), p_2) \preceq D(x, p_1)$. Transitivity of $\preceq$ gives the result. $\square$

Theorem 5.3 (Delegation Non-Amplification). Let $x_0$ be an initial grant. For every $k \geq 0$ and every delegation chain $x_0 \to x_1 \to \cdots \to x_k$: $x_k \preceq x_0$.

Proof. By induction on $k$. Base case $k = 0$: $x_0 \preceq x_0$. Inductive step: assume $x_k \preceq x_0$. By DP3, $x_{k+1} = D(x_k, p_{k+1}) \preceq x_k \preceq x_0$. $\square$

Corollary 5.4 (No Chain Escalation). Under Axioms 3.13 and 3.14, no chain of delegations initiated outside the reasoning state can exceed the originating grant.

Corollary 5.5 (Ambient-Authority Persistence under Delegation). If $X^{\text{amb}}_0 \cap \mathrm{Priv} = \varnothing$ and every new capability is produced by $D$ or $\mathrm{Grant}$, then $X^{\text{amb}}_t \cap \mathrm{Priv} = \varnothing$ for all $t$.

Proof. DP2 holds at $t = 0$ by hypothesis. Each transition either attenuates via $D$ (preserving the invariant by Theorem 5.3) or mints via $\mathrm{Grant}$ (which by Definition 3.5 mints into $X^{\exp}$, not $X^{\text{amb}}$). By induction, the invariant is preserved. $\square$

Remark 5.6. This result is standard attenuation in the capability tradition (Dennis–Van Horn, 1966; Miller, 2006). We state it here for completeness of the ACAH formalization.

