Chapter 7 — The Topological Noninterference Theorem

We now establish the central architectural result. The third draft's Theorem 6.7 was circular: it defined the authority loop as PDP-gated and then proved it was PDP-gated. The correct formulation is a two-run noninterference property in the Goguen–Meseguer tradition.

Definition 7.1 (Low-Integrity Inputs). Let $\mathcal{I}_{\text{low}}$ be the set of inputs labelled low-integrity by the CIL.

Definition 7.2 (Privileged-Effect Trace). For a run $\sigma$ of $\mathfrak{A}$, let $\mathrm{Triv}(\sigma)$ be the sequence of privileged effects realized in $\sigma$.

Definition 7.3 (Ratification Event). A ratification event is an explicit PDP decision that authorizes a privileged effect under a capability minted by $\mathrm{Grant}$ from a policy-derived source.

Axiom 7.4 (Complete Mediation). Every effect passes through the PEP. (Restated from Axiom 3.23.)

Theorem 7.5 (Topological Noninterference). Let $\sigma_1, \sigma_2$ be two runs of $\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{ACAH}}$ that differ only in the values of inputs in $\mathcal{I}_{\text{low}}$. Then

\mathrm{Triv}(\sigma_1) = \mathrm{Triv}(\sigma_2)

except at ratification events.

Proof. Suppose for contradiction that $\mathrm{Triv}(\sigma_1) \neq \mathrm{Triv}(\sigma_2)$ at some non-ratification step. By Axiom 7.4, every effect passes through the PEP. The PEP's output is the PDP's decision (Definition 6.5). The PDP's decision function $\Pi$ takes as input $(p, X, r, C_{\text{integrity}}, \pi, E, \mathrm{Risk}, t)$. The inputs $p, X, r, \pi, E, \mathrm{Risk}, t$ are identical across $\sigma_1$ and $\sigma_2$ by hypothesis. The only possibly differing input is $C_{\text{integrity}}$, which by the CIL's labelling assigns low-integrity labels to inputs in $\mathcal{I}_{\text{low}}$. Since the PDP's decision function does not depend on the content of low-integrity inputs but only on their integrity label, $\Pi$ produces identical decisions across $\sigma_1$ and $\sigma_2$. Hence the PEP produces identical outputs. Contradiction. $\square$

Corollary 7.6 (Reasoning ≠ Authority). In an ACAH topology:

\boxed{\text{Reasoning flow} \neq \text{Authority flow}.}

Corollary 7.7 (Structural Confused Deputy Exclusions). Under complete mediation, no untrusted input processed in the cognitive loop can trigger a privileged execution without an explicit PDP authorization naming a capability minted outside the reasoning state. The confused deputy problem is structurally excluded under the ACAH topology, except where the PDP authorizes an effect whose effect set contains an effect not visible in the approval fields (this gap is the subject of R₁).

Remark 7.8. Corollary 7.7 is weaker than the third draft's "structurally inexpressible" claim. The exclusion holds only for effects visible to the PDP. Effects not visible (per arXiv 2609.38983 and 2609.28586) are the residual R₁.

