Chapter 8 — Sufficiency and Falsification

8.1 The Sufficiency Theorem

Definition 8.1 (Field Map). Define the field map $\phi : \text{Executions} \to \text{Fields}$ by

\phi(e) = (\mathrm{Canonicalize}(\text{args}_e), cwd_e, \mathrm{hash}(\mathrm{env}_e), \mathrm{node}_e, r_e, \mathrm{fb}_e).

Definition 8.2 (Effect Map). Define $\psi : \text{Executions} \to 2^{\mathcal{E}}$ by $\psi(e) = \mathrm{Eff}(e)$.

Theorem 8.3 (Sufficiency). The Verify predicate is sound (i.e., $\mathrm{Verify}(\alpha, e, t, \Lambda_t) = 1 \Rightarrow \mathrm{Eff}(e) \subseteq \mathrm{Eff}(\alpha)$) iff $\phi$ is a sufficient statistic for $\psi$: for all $e_1, e_2$, $\phi(e_1) = \phi(e_2) \Rightarrow \psi(e_1) = \psi(e_2)$.

Proof. ($\Rightarrow$) Suppose Verify is sound. If $\phi(e_1) = \phi(e_2)$, then any $\alpha$ with $\phi(e_1) = \phi(\alpha)$ also has $\phi(e_2) = \phi(\alpha)$, so Verify passes for both, and soundness gives $\mathrm{Eff}(e_1) \subseteq \mathrm{Eff}(\alpha)$ and $\mathrm{Eff}(e_2) \subseteq \mathrm{Eff}(\alpha)$. If $\psi(e_1) \neq \psi(e_2)$, there exists an effect primitive in one but not the other, contradicting the shared containment in $\mathrm{Eff}(\alpha)$. Hence $\psi(e_1) = \psi(e_2)$. ($\Leftarrow$) If $\phi$ is sufficient for $\psi$ and Verify passes, then $\phi(e) = \phi(\alpha)$, so $\psi(e) = \psi(\alpha)$, i.e., $\mathrm{Eff}(e) = \mathrm{Eff}(\alpha) \subseteq \mathrm{Eff}(\alpha)$. $\square$

Corollary 8.4. The operational form of I₅ is sound but incomplete: it is sound iff $\phi$ is sufficient for $\psi$, and it is incomplete whenever $\phi$ does not capture all effect-relevant features.

Remark 8.5. The information limit of arXiv 2609.28586 is precisely the failure of sufficiency: identical policy-visible fields can require different effect-specific decisions. R₁ becomes the problem of characterizing the kernel of $\phi$ and extending it minimally to achieve sufficiency.

8.2 Falsification of the Boolean Form of I₅

Proposition 8.6 (Boolean I₅ is Unsound). Let $\mathrm{Approval}$ be a boolean variable with granularity coarser than the effect set. Then there exists a reachable state $s^* \in \mathrm{Reach}(\mathfrak{A})$ for any architecture using such a boolean approval, with $I_5(s^*) = 0$.

Proof. Witness: CVE-2026-100530. An allow-always approval issued in directory $d_1$ permits execution of an identically-argumented command in directory $d_2$. The NVD description confirms: "OpenClaw versions before 2026.8.1 fail to bind working directory context to reusable exec approvals, allowing approved commands to execute in different directories. Attackers with an allow-always approval can reuse it to run the same command against unreviewed files or repositories with materially different effects". Thus $e_1 = (cmd, d_1)$ and $e_2 = (cmd, d_2)$ share a boolean approval with $\mathrm{Eff}(e_2) \not\subseteq \mathrm{Eff}(e_1)$. Hence boolean I₅ fails. $\square$

Remark 8.7. The unsoundness is due to the approval's granularity (a durable allow-always covering too coarse a class of executions), not due to its boolean-ness. A per-execution boolean bound out of band would be sound. This corrects the overbroad statement of the third draft.

Corollary 8.8. The certificate formulation of I₅ is strictly stronger than the boolean formulation when the certificate granularity is finer than the boolean granularity.

