11.2 Claims and Non-Claims

Claimed. A state-based framework, a capability lattice with non-circular explicit/ambient separation, a non-amplification theorem, a certificate formalism with sufficiency characterization, five invariants as predicates, a topological noninterference theorem, a witness set for reachability, and six formal residuals.

Not claimed. That the invariants are complete or canonical. That ACAH has been implemented. That any prevalence or statistical inference is supported. That the ideal form of I₅ is decidable.

11.3 Research Program

\boxed{\text{Formal Core} \to \text{Sufficiency Kernel} \to \text{Noninterference Extensions} \to \text{Proof Obligations} \to \text{Discharged Theorems}}

11.4 Final Statement

An agentic system is not secured by making its model more intelligent, nor by adding guardrails. It is secured by structuring where authority may flow and by proving that no reasoning — however intelligent — can substitute for authorization.

\underbrace{\mathrm{LLM}}_{\text{proposal}} \neq \underbrace{\mathrm{PDP}}_{\text{authorization}} \neq \underbrace{\mathrm{Broker}}_{\text{capability}} \neq \underbrace{\mathrm{PEP}}_{\text{enforcement}} \neq \underbrace{\mathrm{Execution}}_{\text{effect}}

No prompt can substitute for a proof that $X^{\text{amb}}_t \cap \mathrm{Priv} = \varnothing$.

\boxed{\text{Intelligence proposes. Architecture disposes.}}

