Appendix B — Classical Mathematical Background

B.1 Capability Systems (Dennis–Van Horn, 1966)

Definition B.1 (Capability System). A capability system is a tuple $(O, S, C, R)$ where:

· $O$ is a set of objects,
· $S$ is a set of subjects (processes),
· $C$ is a set of capabilities,
· $R$ is a set of access rights.

A capability is a pair $(o, r) \in O \times R$ held by a subject $s$; possession of the capability authorizes $s$ to exercise $r$ on $o$.

Axiom B.2 (Capability Confinement). A subject may only obtain a capability from another subject that already holds it, or through a designated "capability minting" operation.

Historical note. Dennis and Van Horn introduced the term "capability" as the fundamental unit of authority and argued that confinement is achieved when no capability is passed to an untrusted subject without attenuation.

B.2 Take-Grant Protection Model

Definition B.3 (Take-Grant Graph). A take-grant protection model is a directed graph $G = (V, E)$ where $V$ is the set of subjects and objects and $E \subseteq V \times V \times \{\text{take}, \text{grant}, \text{read}, \text{write}, \ldots\}$ labels edges with rights.

Theorem B.4 (Take-Grant Reachability). In a take-grant model with take and grant rights, the set of subjects that can obtain a right $r$ on object $o$ is computable in linear time in $|V| + |E|$.

Relevance to ACAH. The delegation operator $D$ and the minting operator $\mathrm{Grant}$ are the only edges permitted to propagate authority; all others are restricted to non-privileged data flow.

B.3 HRU Safety (Harrison–Ruzzo–Ullman, 1976)

Theorem B.5 (HRU Safety Undecidability). The safety problem for the Harrison–Ruzzo–Ullman access matrix model — determining whether a given right can ever leak to a given subject — is undecidable in general.

Implication. No general-purpose agent architecture can decide, by static analysis alone, whether every possible delegation chain preserves the ambient-authority invariant. This is why ACAH imposes the invariant by construction (DP2) rather than by verification.

B.4 Denning Lattice Model

Definition B.6 (Security Lattice). A security lattice is a bounded lattice $(\mathcal{L}, \leq, \sqcup, \sqcap)$ of security levels.

Axiom B.7 (Non-Interference by Lattice). In the Denning model, a subject at level $\ell$ may read objects at levels $\leq \ell$ and write objects at levels $\geq \ell$. These restrictions define a non-interference boundary.

Relevance to ACAH. The capability lattice $(\mathcal{X}, \preceq)$ is a Denning-style lattice with the interpretation "less authority" rather than "higher secrecy." The facet-wise order is the generalization of the scalar order in the classical model.

B.5 Goguen–Meseguer Noninterference

Definition B.8 (Noninterference). Let $H$ be a set of high inputs and $L$ a set of low inputs. A system satisfies noninterference iff for any two runs $\sigma_1, \sigma_2$ that agree on $L$, the low-observable behavior of $\sigma_1$ and $\sigma_2$ is identical.

Theorem B.9 (Unwinding — Goguen–Meseguer). A system satisfies noninterference iff there exists an unwinding relation on states satisfying the output consistency, weak step consistency, and local respect conditions.

Relevance to ACAH. Theorem 7.5 is the ACAH instantiation of Definition B.8 with:

· $H = \mathcal{I}_{\text{high}}$ (high-integrity inputs),
· $L = \mathcal{I}_{\text{low}}$ (low-integrity inputs),
· low-observable behavior = $\mathrm{Triv}$ (privileged-effect trace).

The unwinding relation is the field-map equivalence $\phi(e_1) = \phi(e_2)$.

B.6 Anderson's Reference Monitor (1972)

Definition B.10 (Reference Monitor). A reference monitor is an abstract machine that mediates all access requests and decides them according to a policy.

Axiom B.11 (Reference Monitor Criteria).

1. Complete mediation: every access is checked.
2. Tamperproofness: the monitor cannot be modified by untrusted code.
3. Verifiability: the monitor is small enough to be analyzed.

Relevance to ACAH. Axioms 3.23–3.25 are the formal statements of B.11. Theorem 7.5 requires Axiom 3.23; without complete mediation, the proof fails.

B.7 Macaroons (Birgisson et al., NDSS 2014)

Definition B.12 (Macaroon). A macaroon is a tuple $(l, c_1, \ldots, c_n, \sigma)$ where $l$ is a location, $c_i$ are caveats, and $\sigma$ is a chained HMAC. Verification checks the chained HMAC and evaluates each caveat against the request context.

Relevance to ACAH. The approval certificate $\alpha$ generalizes a macaroon by:

1. Replacing caveats with typed fields (cwd, env hash, file binding),
2. Adding a ledger reference for nonce-replay prevention,
3. Requiring effect-set containment rather than caveat satisfaction.

B.8 Object-Capability Model (Miller, 2006)

Definition B.13 (Object-Capability). In an object-capability system, capabilities are unforgeable references to objects. Authority is exercised by invoking a method on a held reference. No ambient authority exists; all authority is passed as an argument.

Theorem B.14 (Confused Deputy Resolution — Miller). In a pure object-capability system, the confused deputy problem cannot arise: the deputy must be explicitly handed the authority it exercises on the requester's behalf, and cannot exercise authority it merely holds.

Relevance to ACAH. Theorem 5.3 (Delegation Non-Amplification) and Corollary 7.7 (Structural Confused Deputy Exclusions) are the ACAH versions of Theorem B.14. The distinction is that ACAH admits an OS-level ambient authority (host process privileges) that must be neutralized by DP2, whereas pure object-capability systems exclude it by construction.

