Appendix H — Sufficiency Theory

H.1 Sufficient Statistics

Definition H.1 (Sufficient Statistic). Let $\phi : \Omega \to \Phi$ and $\psi : \Omega \to \Psi$. Then $\phi$ is sufficient for $\psi$ iff $\phi(\omega_1) = \phi(\omega_2) \Rightarrow \psi(\omega_1) = \psi(\omega_2)$ for all $\omega_1, \omega_2 \in \Omega$.

Lemma H.2 (Factorization). $\phi$ is sufficient for $\psi$ iff there exists a function $g : \Phi \to \Psi$ with $\psi = g \circ \phi$.

Proof. ($\Rightarrow$) Define $g(\phi(\omega)) = \psi(\omega)$. Well-defined by sufficiency. ($\Leftarrow$) If $\phi(\omega_1) = \phi(\omega_2)$, then $\psi(\omega_1) = g(\phi(\omega_1)) = g(\phi(\omega_2)) = \psi(\omega_2)$. $\square$

H.2 Information Limit

Theorem H.3 (Information Limit — arXiv 2609.28586 restated). If there exist $e_1, e_2$ with $\phi(e_1) = \phi(e_2)$ and $\psi(e_1) \neq \psi(e_2)$, then no record-only policy over $\phi$-fields can guarantee both soundness and completeness for all effect classes.

Proof. Soundness requires $\mathrm{Eff}(e) \subseteq \mathrm{Eff}(\alpha)$ whenever $\phi(e) = \phi(\alpha)$. If $\phi(e_1) = \phi(e_2)$ and $\psi(e_1) \neq \psi(e_2)$, then a single $\alpha$ with $\phi(\alpha) = \phi(e_1)$ must both contain and not contain the distinguishing effect. Contradiction. $\square$

H.3 Minimal Extension

Definition H.4 (Minimal Sufficient Extension). A field map $\phi^*$ extends $\phi$ (written $\phi \sqsubseteq \phi^*$) if $\phi(e_1) = \phi(e_2) \Rightarrow \phi^*(e_1) = \phi^*(e_2)$. A minimal sufficient extension is a $\phi^* \sqsupseteq \phi$ with $\phi^*$ sufficient for $\psi$ and no proper sub-extension $\phi' \sqsubsetneq \phi^*$ sufficient.

Problem H.5 (R₁ restated). Characterize the minimal sufficient extension of $\phi$ for the effect map $\psi$.

