Problem L.2. Define $\mathrm{Allow}_\sigma : \text{Trajectories} \to \{\mathrm{Allow}, \mathrm{Deny}\}$ such that trajectory-level invariants (no slow-burn privilege escalation, no compliance drift) are enforced.

Formalization. Let $\sigma = (q_0, q_1, \ldots, q_n)$ be a trajectory. Define

\mathrm{Allow}_\sigma(\sigma) = 1 \iff \forall i:\ \Pi(s_i, q_i) = \mathrm{Allow} \land \Phi(\sigma_{0..i})

where $\Phi$ is a trajectory invariant (to be defined).

L.3 R₃ — Multi-Agent Amplification

Problem L.3. Formalize the failure of composition:

\exists A, B, C:\ P(A) \land P(B) \land P(C) \land \neg P(A \to B \to C).

Direction. Use PEDIGREE's per-hop delegation and the monotone narrowing of Theorem 5.3 to characterize when composition is safe.

L.4 R₄ — Context Provenance without Injection

Problem L.4. Characterize the class $\mathcal{M}$ of context manipulations such that:

\forall m \in \mathcal{M}:\ m \text{ violates context integrity without violating } I_1 \ldots I_5.

Direction. Semantic framing, selective retrieval, ordering effects, representational manipulation.

L.5 R₅ — Natural-Language Skill Semantics

Problem L.5. Define a relation $\mathrm{Match} : \text{Descriptions} \times \mathcal{X}_{\text{scope}} \to [0, 1]$ such that high match implies the skill's authorized scope covers its declared semantics.

L.6 R₆ — Authority Observability

Problem L.6. Define $\mathrm{Reconstruct} : \text{Executions} \to \mathrm{AuthorityProvenance}$ and determine its totality.

Formalization. Characterize the class $\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{obs}} \subseteq \text{Executions}$ on which $\mathrm{Reconstruct}$ is total.

